<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trelleborg (Slagelse)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trelleborg is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> ring fortress near Slagelse on Zealand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, built around 980 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>. Its circular rampart, divided into four quarters with uniform longhouses, follows the same plan as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyrkat">Fyrkat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aggersborg">Aggersborg</a> and the other Danish ring forts. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the Viking Age ring fortresses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trelleborg-slagelse.jpg" alt="Trelleborg (Slagelse)" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thue C. Leibrandt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trelleborg_airphoto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>When it was built, the fortress stood on a peninsula reaching into a swamp between two rivers, the Vårby Å and the Tude Å. The swamp was linked to the Great Belt by a lake that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> ships could navigate, and the fort may have commanded the sea traffic between Zealand and Funen.</p><p>The circular main castle was enclosed by a rampart 5 m high, 17.5 m wide at the base and 137 m across, faced with oak and filled with loam and stones. Two roads crossing at right angles led to four gates, and each quarter held four almost identical longhouses with bulging walls and a central hall of 18 by 8 m.</p><p>Unlike the other ring castles, Trelleborg had a bailey with its own rampart and 14 longhouses whose axes pointed to the centre of the main castle. Beside it lay a cemetery of 135 graves, mostly of young men. The fortress may have held some 1,300 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dendrochronology shows that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> was felled in the autumn of 980 and probably used in the spring of 981; earlier datings had put the castle near the year 1000. The short building time and the lack of any sign of repair suggest the buildings were used for perhaps 10 to 15 years, and the outer eastern rampart was probably never finished.</p><p>Excavations from 1934 to 1942 found mostly everyday objects, but 19 arrowheads driven into the ramparts and gates, three mass graves of men with wounds from close combat, and traces of fire point to an attack. Strontium analysis showed that many of the dead came from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and they are thought to have been foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> serving as guards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Trelleborg is the best preserved of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> ring fortresses. Post-glacial rebound has shrunk the swamp to a small bog and the rivers to narrow streams. A longhouse was reconstructed on the site in 1948, though later study has questioned its design, and since 1995 the Trelleborg Museum has run the site as an open-air museum, with other finds kept at the Nationalmuseet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trelleborg (Slagelse)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harald also built the bridge at Ravning Enge and the circular fortresses of <b>Trelleborg</b>, Fyrkat and Aggersborg, which tree-ring dating places around 980; according to Adam of Bremen his son Sweyn Forkbeard rebelled and drove him into exile, where he died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
